WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.470 align:start position:0%
 
this is a film about  ordinary normal people living in present  day who are drafted to fight a war  that's happening 40 years in the future  you  are our last  hope  and when we get literally dropped into  the future it's insanely intense  and when we first meet the white spikes  it's pretty gnarly  you know in the script the creature was  called a white spike and that was almost  the entirety of the description and so i  think we deduced that it needed to be  white and then it needed to somehow have  a split spike we really started with the  description of what the creatures needed  to do in the story but we really sort of  turned the artist loose and you know it  was very challenging because we're  trying to find a creature that was new  and fresh and that we hadn't seen before  and yet also had all of these very  lethal aspects to it but that really was  only the beginning of it you know then  there's a three-dimensional component of  it we have to understand how the  creature moves how it has its weight and  so we moved into a 3d environment with  that design once we did all that then we  had to do motion tests and understand  how it moved and how it could jump and  how fast it could move realistically  one of the things chris always told us  is you want to feel like just looking at  it is going to hurt you like if you just  brush up against this thing or if you're  too close to it you know something bad  is going to happen and so all of that  texture and sort of rotten quality of  the flesh and the fissures and the  cracks and the flakes you know all of  that we really dug into we took a lot of  inspiration in the design from lethal  creatures in our world  we looked at all different kinds of skin  surfaces rhinoceros skin hippopotamus  we looked at cheetahs and leopards for  the way that their joints allow them to  run quickly we looked at snakes for the  way their jaw might unhinge  and one of the things that you know  we're really proud of and that i think  the animators just did an incredible job  on is the facial presence of the  creature you know it has a bony face it  doesn't have a lot of flexibility and so  it doesn't emote in sort of the  traditional ways furrowing its brows  squinting its eyes or turning its  corners of its mouth up and down and so  you know with those limited controls the  animators were really  able to create a connection between the  audience and the creature and the other  characters in the movie and the  creatures and i think that's quite an  achievement for them yeah  it was a steep learning curve for our  animation team to really execute what  needed to be still convincing and  believable creature but outside of the  normal rule set the motion editing team  utilizes the upfront work that was  invested in our animation department to  really leverage the amount of different  type of behaviors whether it's leaping  jumping attacking we use this library  really to create like a very bespoke  approach to  animate hundreds of them in a very  effective and efficient way  to propagate into  large scenes and settings  one of our interesting challenges on  this movie is we had a number of  different vendors and we had to have the  appearance and the performance of the  creatures be the same throughout the  movie so we had weta doing the final  model of the creature and then they had  also done all of our motion tests and so  we shared that model and we shared those  motion tests with each of the different  teams including luma and they were able  to you know really kind of deliver the  kind of animation style and performance  you know that we needed and that matched  the other performances in the movie  all right dan i'm gonna need you to head  to the row of biomedical freezers along  the south wall in the lab  the stairwell was important because it's  really the first time that any of the  characters encounter the white spikes  one of the things i really wanted out of  the scene was to kind of destabilize the  audience a little bit so turn all the  music off like just had like natural  sounds going  as the sounds of the world kind of fade  away we lock in on dan's breathing and  that we're just isolated with him  so hollowness the echoes all this stuff  exemplify how they're feeling  you want it to feel like a haunted house  so it was set up to fulfill the  uneasiness and creepiness and the  audience they've done the math at this  point that the creature could kind of be  anywhere and could come out of anywhere  so they're really on pins and needles  and that's like to me that's the perfect  way of getting the audience like right  there leaning in  what the scene needed to do is needed to  show that they had some kind of  intelligence and some sort of  communication you know this scene really  was white spikes 101.  these white spikes have a language chris  said they have like a guttural clicking  something in the deep parts of their  abdomen their throat that generates this  clicking type of sound that they use to  bark orders to communicate across  distances  so we dug through tons of pack animals  wolves birds  dolphins  a recording of a pool sweeper it would  make this insanely gross kind of viscous  suction sound that then he would use to  envelop the beginning and ends of these  white spikes vocal phrases  when we're doing things like this and  we're working with the actors on set you  know there's no creature there we had a  puppet or we had a stand-in we had  something sometimes we'd have a stunt  person in there with the puppet acting  out the creature but it's always messy  you know then performers won't do  exactly what you expect them to do or  the camera operator won't do exactly  what you expect them to do  instead of looking at that as a mistake  we embraced it you know we found a way  to how do we justify this how do we use  this cool unexpected move you know to  reverse engineer into something that the  creature does that's unexpected there's  always this element of spontaneous  organic you know stuff just made the  thing feel more alive to me and that's  what i really loved about it  oh [ __ ]  oh  [ __ ] what you're seeing is some of it's  the set but most of it is a digital  creation done by jamie and his team the  other thing i think that the close  proximity the stairwell brought to the  scene was lighting the muzzle flashes  became a lighting source in and of  themselves  so i think that helped add to the  element of surprise and a lot of the  performance of the creatures  go  particularly in the hallway we had an  opportunity where we really wanted to  illustrate the relentlessness of the  creatures eventually culminating with  the moment with the axe you know we had  a really interesting animation challenge  there to understand you know that this  thing was heavy was being wounded and  then yet with every wound it was  changing its style so that it could  continue to move forward and threaten  our character you know until eventually  chris gets the upper hand so that was a  really fun challenge and something that  we worked very hard and had a lot of  back and forth on to make sure that we  really understood the power the  relentlessness of the creature  next all the bellies so only little part  on that body let's go  let's go overall the main thing that  attracted me to this project was the  story you know and chris wanting to tell  a very human story about this  relationship between  father and daughter and surrounding an  intimate story like that with visual  effects is not something that we see all  the time so that was a really great  opportunity and having the white spikes  be a character in the movie you know  really be the antagonist in in the movie  and have it have the creatures have  different forms with the males and the  females it just gave us so many  opportunities to bring those creatures  to life and to be such a big part of  that story so it's really satisfying  [Music]

